$Id: README,v 1.1 2011/05/08 02:20:53 drmiller Exp $

This is the start of a Wang 600 Simulator, using actual microcode from a Wang 600.

By default it uses a Java front-end. That can be disabled with "-g".

S basic help message is displayed when "w600-sim -h" is run.

Defaults work with included files, namely the microcode image in "wang600.rom".

Option -t will enable trace, with the expected volumes of output.

Option -i enables "interactive mode" whereby the simulator starts in command mode.
"help" will do the obvious. "go" will start executing the microcode image.
Without a GUI, it is a very crude interface.

Currently, the GUI requires all the *.class files in the main directory, plus the
GIF images in the "icons" subdirectory. The GUI has a "test" mode, which is what
runs when the java file "w600_fe.class" is run outside of the simulator.
For example, by "java w600_fe".

There is a simple disassembler for the microcode, "w600-dasm". It uses the 
same format as the trace facility in the simulator, except there is no
hardware state information.

Some basic diagrams and notes on the hardware are in "wang600-block-diag.pdf".
